(a) Upon presentation of identification to the innkeeper, business owner, property owner, wholesale alcohol dealer, retail alcohol dealer, alcohol license owner, telephone company, insurance company, financial institution, or any other person or business relevant to the provisions of this title (hereinafter collectively "business"), any employee or agent of the City is hereby authorized to make inspections of a business's books, papers, records, financial reports, computer media, data storage media, equipment and other facilities in order to verify the accuracy of any revenues, financial reports, financial returns, documents, and taxes, for the purposes of auditing said financial records to determine if there are any monies owed by the business to the City and to determine whether the business is in compliance with the requirements of this chapter.
(b) Inspections shall be carried out during regular business hours, unless an emergency exists. The inspection warrant shall be effective for the entire time specified therein, but not for a period of more than 14 calendar days, unless extended or renewed by the judicial officer who signed and issued the original inspection warrant, upon satisfying himself that such extension or renewal is in the public interest. After the expiration of such time, the inspection warrant, unless executed, is void. The City's employees and agents may carry out such inspection pursuant to the inspection warrant each and every day during the 14-day period without requiring the City to secure any additional inspection warrants.
(c) The Chief Financial Officer, Director of Finance or their designee, in addition to other procedures provided by law, may obtain an inspection warrant under the conditions specified in this Section. The inspection warrant shall authorize the Chief Financial Officer, Director of Finance or their designee to conduct a search or inspection of property without the consent of the person whose property is to be searched or inspected, under the conditions set out in this section.
(d) Inspection warrants may be issued by any judge of the Municipal Court when the issuing judge is satisfied that the following conditions are met:
(1) The person seeking the inspection warrant must establish under oath or affirmation that there is probable cause for believing that the business is not in compliance with the requirements of this title;
(2) The issuing judge determines that the issuance of the warrant is authorized by this title and all other applicable law;
(3) The inspection warrant is attached to a supporting affidavit which particularly describes the property to be inspected and particularly describes the purpose for which the inspection is to be made;
(4) The inspection warrant describes, either directly or by reference to the affidavit, the property upon which the inspection is to occur and is sufficiently accurate that the executor of the warrant and the owner or possessor of the property can reasonably determine from the warrant the property for which the warrant authorizes a search or inspection;
(5) The warrant indicates the conditions, objects, activities, or circumstances that the search or inspection is intended to check or reveal; and
(6) The warrant refers in general terms, to the provisions of the code or state law sought to be enforced.
(e) It shall be unlawful for any business, operator or employee of the business to refuse to allow an inspection pursuant to an inspection warrant that has been issued in accordance with this section.
(Ord. No. 1404, § 2, 11-20-2024)

